Virtual machine tests

The tests in this directory boot a real machine. They are not part of the
validation gates, because they need QEMU with UEFI firmware, a kernel and
disk images.

laft-boot

The unit tests of crates/laft-boot cover its decisions: the kernel command
line, laft-boot.json, mount options, superblock probing, device lookup in a
fake sysfs, generation selection, boot counting, the fallback with a power
loss after each of its steps, the boot menu rewrite, the order and flags of
the mount plans, and the root layout check. One test runs the generation
mount, the read-only /laft and the move of the kernel filesystems in a user
and mount namespace. What remains needs PID 1 on a real kernel and will be
tested here:

- Plain boot from an ext4 root without LUKS reaches a getty.
  /run/laft/system and /run/laft/booted point at mounts/<N>, and
  /proc/self/mountinfo shows /laft as ro,nosuid,nodev and
  /run/laft/mounts/<N> as ro,nodev without nosuid.
- Roots on xfs, btrfs and f2fs boot, and so does a separate /laft.
- A LUKS2 root boots after the passphrase is typed on the console. Two
  wrong passphrases followed by the right one boot, and three wrong ones
  drop to the emergency shell.
- For images made by each mkfs tool, UUID probing finds the UUID that the
  filesystem's own tools report.
- With the root disk detached, the shell appears after root_wait seconds
  and lists the block devices seen.
- A generation whose init fails is booted the configured number of times,
  then the machine falls back to the last good generation, in place when
  the kernel and initramfs are the same and through a rewritten
  default_entry and a reboot otherwise. In both cases default_entry names
  the last good generation afterwards, and a second reboot boots it
  again.
- Cutting the power while laft-boot falls back, before the boot menu is
  rewritten and again before boot.json is saved, still ends in the last
  good generation, with the failed one marked bad and one fallback event.
- laft.break=premount and laft.break=preswitch reach the shell, and
  leaving it continues the boot.
- fsck exit code 2 reboots, and 4 drops to the shell with the repair hint.
- A panic in laft-boot reaches the shell and then reboots.

Each test builds a disk image in a temporary directory, writes the ESP and
the root from a Laft generation, boots it with QEMU and OVMF, and drives
the serial console with scripts that wait for its output and type the
replies. The result is read from the console and from the image after
shutdown.

laft-enter

The unit tests of crates/laft-enter cover its decisions: the command line
and the view name rule, the limits on arguments and environment, the
owner, mode, type and size checks on view.json and privileged.json, their
format, name and version matching, the links /run/laft/system and
/laft/views/<name>/current, authorization by uid and gid, the order of the
privilege drop and its verification, the environment handed to the
command, the default shell, the path search, the working directory
fallback, the caller's umask for the command and the exit statuses 126
and 127. An integration test checks
that the binary refuses to run without the setuid bit. The mount sequence
is tested in crates/laft-sandbox inside a user namespace. What remains
needs a setuid root binary on a booted generation and will be tested here,
each as an unprivileged user unless stated otherwise:

- A user listed neither in a view's allowed uids nor through a group in
  its allowed gids is refused with exit status 1 and the message
  "laft-enter: uid <uid> may not enter view "<name>"". A user in a listed
  group is allowed, and so is root, who stays root inside.
- Inside a view, /usr shows the view, /etc/static shows the system's
  configuration, /proc/self/mountinfo shows the view ro,nosuid,nodev, and
  the host's mount table is unchanged afterwards. A USB drive mounted on
  the host while the session runs appears inside.
- In a view that inherits the system, doas true works. In a view that pins
  a different linux-pam, /usr/bin/doas is the view's own unprivileged file
  and doas fails without raising privileges.
- With /laft/views/dev/current pointing at a version other than the one
  privileged.json records for dev (arranged as root), entering prints the
  note that privileged programs are unavailable, binds none, and works.
- LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/evil.so laft-enter dev true never creates the marker
  file evil.so writes as root. MIMALLOC_ variables change nothing but the
  allocator's own diagnostics.
- Replacing /laft/views/dev/current with a symlink to a directory outside
  /laft/views, or view.json or privileged.json with a symlink, a FIFO, a
  file owned by another user or a group writable file (each done as root)
  makes laft-enter refuse with exit status 1.
- laft gc does not remove a view version while a laft-enter session for
  it runs, and removes it once the session and every process that
  inherited the lock have ended.
- A caller that closes descriptors 0 to 2, passes extra descriptors, sets
  RLIMIT_NOFILE to 3, ignores or blocks every signal, sets umask 0, or
  starts from a directory it cannot access either enters correctly or
  exits 1 with nothing mounted on the host. The extra descriptors are not
  open in the command, but the view.json lock descriptor is.
- Inside the session, /proc/self/status shows the caller's ids in every
  slot, no capabilities, and NoNewPrivs 0. Sending signals to laft-enter
  during setup never leaves a mount behind on the host.
- laft-enter dev missing-program exits 127, and laft-enter dev /etc/passwd
  exits 126.
- Nested use works: laft-enter other inside a session of dev mounts other
  and binds the system's privileged files.
